            <ArticleTitle>Unusual Complication after Intramedullary Nailing of an Intertrochanteric Fracture.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>337-340</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1615/JLongTermEffMedImplants.2017019151</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Intramedullary nails have gained popularity for the treatment of intertrochanteric fractures. Cephalic screw cutout, non-union, nail breakage, intraoperative fractures, and peri-implant fractures are complications of these implants and have been well described. This case report describes an unusual complication in an 89-year-old woman presenting with a femoral neck fracture with an intramedullary nail in place without implant failure or non-union after having been successfully treated three years previously for an intertrochanteric fracture.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
